1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,775
The Kopitiam's Two Eggs: The
Breakfast That Outlived a Health Scare

2
00:00:04,775 --> 00:00:08,660
Order breakfast in a Singapore
kopitiam and it arrives in

3
00:00:08,740 --> 00:00:11,721
a form that has barely
changed in a century:

4
00:00:11,801 --> 00:00:16,521
two soft-boiled eggs cracked into
a saucer, with dark soy sauce and a

5
00:00:16,601 --> 00:00:20,775
shake of white pepper; kaya
toast; and a cup of kopi or tea.

6
00:00:20,775 --> 00:00:24,514
In 2019 the National
Heritage Board listed this

7
00:00:24,594 --> 00:00:28,008
"Traditional Breakfast
of Kaya and Kopi" in

8
00:00:28,088 --> 00:00:32,475
Singapore's inventory of
intangible cultural heritage.

9
00:00:32,475 --> 00:00:36,803
Yet for roughly fifty of the years
that Singaporeans were eating it,

10
00:00:36,883 --> 00:00:39,850
the two eggs in the saucer
sat at the centre of

11
00:00:39,930 --> 00:00:43,091
one of modern nutrition's
longest-running scares —

12
00:00:43,171 --> 00:00:47,450
officially limited, warned against,
and portioned out by the week.

13
00:00:47,450 --> 00:00:51,150
The guidance swung, swung
back, and swung again.

14
00:00:51,150 --> 00:00:53,825
The breakfast never moved.

15
00:00:53,825 --> 00:00:56,850
The eggs are not boiled, exactly.

16
00:00:56,850 --> 00:01:00,335
They are steeped: room-temperature
eggs go into a covered

17
00:01:00,415 --> 00:01:03,650
pot or enamel mug, boiling
water is poured over them,

18
00:01:03,730 --> 00:01:07,715
and they sit in the falling heat
— home cooks time it at anything

19
00:01:07,795 --> 00:01:11,593
from five and a half to ten
minutes, depending on the vessel —

20
00:01:11,673 --> 00:01:15,050
until the whites are just
set and the yolks still run.

21
00:01:15,050 --> 00:01:19,000
Cracked into a saucer, they
are seasoned the local way.

22
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:23,324
The British breakfast this
descends from used salt and pepper;

23
00:01:23,404 --> 00:01:25,881
Hainanese cooks substituted dark soy

24
00:01:25,961 --> 00:01:29,300
sauce for the salt, and
the substitution stuck.

25
00:01:29,300 --> 00:01:32,275
Why two eggs, and not one or three?

26
00:01:32,275 --> 00:01:36,650
The honest answer is that
nobody ever wrote it down.

27
00:01:36,650 --> 00:01:40,413
The Heritage Board's listing
records "two soft boiled

28
00:01:40,492 --> 00:01:43,900
eggs" as the form of the
dish, and stops there.

29
00:01:43,900 --> 00:01:47,565
The explanations that
circulate — one egg looks mean,

30
00:01:47,645 --> 00:01:52,025
a pair balances the toast — are
folk reasoning after the fact.

31
00:01:52,025 --> 00:01:56,601
The two-egg serving is a documented
custom with no documented origin,

32
00:01:56,681 --> 00:01:59,907
which may be the most
Singaporean thing about it:

33
00:01:59,987 --> 00:02:04,575
it does not need a reason, because
it has always been done this way.

34
00:02:04,575 --> 00:02:08,440
The toast beside the eggs is
charcoal-grilled, crusts trimmed,

35
00:02:08,520 --> 00:02:12,894
spread with kaya and butter — or
margarine; the official sources split

36
00:02:12,974 --> 00:02:17,348
on that too, the Heritage Board's
account saying margarine where other

37
00:02:17,428 --> 00:02:21,500
descriptions say butter, and
diners have never much cared which.

38
00:02:21,500 --> 00:02:24,963
And the drink is ordered
in a vocabulary of its own:

39
00:02:25,043 --> 00:02:30,073
kopi with condensed milk, kopi-o black
with sugar, kopi-o kosong with none,

40
00:02:30,153 --> 00:02:34,366
and the adjustments — siu dai
for less sugar, ga dai for more —

41
00:02:34,446 --> 00:02:38,125
that regulars issue as
reflexively as their own names.

42
00:02:38,125 --> 00:02:41,870
The kopitiam — the word
marries Malay kopi, coffee,

43
00:02:41,950 --> 00:02:46,225
to Hokkien tiam, shop — was
largely a Hainanese creation.

44
00:02:46,225 --> 00:02:49,810
Hainanese migrants were
in Singapore from 1821,

45
00:02:49,890 --> 00:02:53,318
but they arrived later
than the other Chinese

46
00:02:53,398 --> 00:02:57,375
dialect groups and found
many trades already taken.

47
00:02:57,375 --> 00:03:01,057
Many went into service as
cooks, waiters and kitchen

48
00:03:01,138 --> 00:03:04,169
hands in European and
Peranakan households,

49
00:03:04,249 --> 00:03:07,859
on ships and in hotels —
acquiring, in the process,

50
00:03:07,939 --> 00:03:12,425
exactly the skills a Western-style
breakfast trade would need.

51
00:03:12,425 --> 00:03:16,108
Between the 1920s and
the 1950s they made the

52
00:03:16,188 --> 00:03:20,958
coffee shop their niche, and
after 1945, with rents cheap,

53
00:03:21,038 --> 00:03:24,550
kopitiams became common across the island.

54
00:03:24,550 --> 00:03:29,700
The documented pioneer still
stands at 67 Killiney Road.

55
00:03:29,700 --> 00:03:33,545
Kheng Hoe Heng Coffeeshop
was founded there in late

56
00:03:33,625 --> 00:03:36,085
1919 by the Hainanese Foo family,

57
00:03:36,165 --> 00:03:41,475
known for charcoal-grilled toast
and coffee brewed the Hainanese way.

58
00:03:41,475 --> 00:03:46,023
In 1992 a former broker, Woon
Tek Seng, bought it for 800,000

59
00:03:46,103 --> 00:03:49,893
Singapore dollars and
renamed it Killiney Kopitiam;

60
00:03:49,973 --> 00:03:55,356
three Hainanese staff from the old
shop stayed on to pass on the skills,

61
00:03:55,436 --> 00:03:58,922
and in 2008 the shop
entered the Singapore Book

62
00:03:59,002 --> 00:04:03,175
of Records as the oldest
existing Hainanese coffeeshop.

63
00:04:03,175 --> 00:04:06,556
The other founding name
took the opposite path,

64
00:04:06,636 --> 00:04:08,550
from one stall to a chain.

65
00:04:08,550 --> 00:04:12,744
Loi Ah Koon arrived from
Hainan in 1926, aged fifteen,

66
00:04:12,824 --> 00:04:17,493
worked as a coffee-stall
assistant, and set up his own stall

67
00:04:17,573 --> 00:04:22,004
at Telok Ayer Basin; the
business was formally registered

68
00:04:22,084 --> 00:04:26,200
in 1944 — the year Ya Kun
still prints on its signs.

69
00:04:26,200 --> 00:04:28,974
His wife, who joined him in 1936,

70
00:04:29,054 --> 00:04:33,550
perfected the kaya recipe
the chain still trades on.

71
00:04:33,550 --> 00:04:36,448
Ya Kun has since gone international —

72
00:04:36,528 --> 00:04:40,150
the same set is served
from Jakarta to Tokyo.

73
00:04:40,150 --> 00:04:44,854
One Tokyo outlet opened in 2021
in the Kasumigaseki Building

74
00:04:44,934 --> 00:04:50,355
(it has since closed), and that
building carries a personal footnote:

75
00:04:50,435 --> 00:04:54,900
it is where I once worked,
during my AOTS time in Japan.

76
00:04:54,900 --> 00:04:58,705
Loi Ah Koon, roasting beans
over firewood at Telok Ayer,

77
00:04:58,785 --> 00:05:02,600
could not have imagined his
breakfast turning up there.

78
00:05:02,600 --> 00:05:06,690
Kaya is an egg jam: eggs,
coconut milk, sugar and pandan,

79
00:05:06,770 --> 00:05:11,525
cooked slowly into a spread. (The
word is also Malay for "rich".)

80
00:05:11,525 --> 00:05:16,301
It comes in two regional styles —
a green kaya coloured by pandan,

81
00:05:16,381 --> 00:05:19,906
associated with Peranakan
makers, and a brown one

82
00:05:19,986 --> 00:05:24,400
coloured by caramelised sugar,
the Hainanese kopitiam style.

83
00:05:24,400 --> 00:05:28,019
Where it came from is a
genuine scholarly argument,

84
00:05:28,099 --> 00:05:31,000
and the post owes the reader both sides.

85
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:34,420
The older, popular
account makes kaya a local

86
00:05:34,500 --> 00:05:37,142
adaptation of a Portuguese egg jam,

87
00:05:37,222 --> 00:05:41,500
coconut milk standing in for
the dairy of the original.

88
00:05:41,500 --> 00:05:45,528
The revisionist account, argued
by the food historian Khir

89
00:05:45,608 --> 00:05:50,416
Johari and by a peer-reviewed article
in the journal Sejarah in 2024,

90
00:05:50,496 --> 00:05:54,312
holds that the Malay serikaya
family of egg-and-coconut

91
00:05:54,392 --> 00:05:58,208
confections predates the
Portuguese in Southeast Asia —

92
00:05:58,287 --> 00:06:03,175
and may have travelled the other
way, from Melaka to Goa to Portugal.

93
00:06:03,175 --> 00:06:06,293
The evidence stands in
print on both sides;

94
00:06:06,373 --> 00:06:09,200
the jam, like the eggs, is unbothered.

95
00:06:09,200 --> 00:06:13,150
The case against the egg
was built in America.

96
00:06:13,150 --> 00:06:18,475
In 1958 the researcher Ancel Keys
launched his Seven Countries Study,

97
00:06:18,555 --> 00:06:21,843
the foundation of the
diet-heart hypothesis

98
00:06:21,923 --> 00:06:26,075
linking dietary fat and
cholesterol to heart disease.

99
00:06:26,075 --> 00:06:30,288
A decade later, in 1968, the
American Heart Association

100
00:06:30,368 --> 00:06:34,580
issued the recommendation
that started the egg's exile:

101
00:06:34,660 --> 00:06:38,795
less than 300 milligrams of
dietary cholesterol a day,

102
00:06:38,875 --> 00:06:42,075
and no more than three whole eggs a week.

103
00:06:42,075 --> 00:06:44,944
It was the first time a single everyday

104
00:06:45,024 --> 00:06:48,200
food had been portioned out like medicine.

105
00:06:48,200 --> 00:06:52,346
The US Senate's Dietary Goals
echoed the limits in 1977;

106
00:06:52,426 --> 00:06:58,005
Time magazine put cholesterol on its
cover in March 1984; and by 1995 every

107
00:06:58,085 --> 00:07:01,703
major American authority
— the Heart Association,

108
00:07:01,783 --> 00:07:08,570
the national cholesterol programme, the federal
dietary guidelines, the food label itself —

109
00:07:08,650 --> 00:07:12,800
had converged on the same
figure: 300 milligrams a day.

110
00:07:12,800 --> 00:07:16,250
Now weigh the saucer against that budget.

111
00:07:16,250 --> 00:07:22,026
The cholesterol in one egg is itself
a number that will not sit still:

112
00:07:22,106 --> 00:07:27,547
186 milligrams in the current US
Department of Agriculture figure,

113
00:07:27,627 --> 00:07:33,822
213 in its older one, about 200 in a
2021 Singapore Medical Journal review,

114
00:07:33,902 --> 00:07:37,750
240 in the Singapore
Heart Foundation's table.

115
00:07:37,750 --> 00:07:39,800
But take any of them.

116
00:07:39,800 --> 00:07:45,086
Two kopitiam eggs deliver somewhere
between 372 and 480 milligrams —

117
00:07:45,166 --> 00:07:51,005
which is to say, the traditional serving,
eaten before nine in the morning,

118
00:07:51,085 --> 00:07:55,425
meets or exceeds the entire
day's allowance on its own.

119
00:07:55,425 --> 00:07:59,673
For fifty years, by the official
arithmetic of the scare years,

120
00:07:59,753 --> 00:08:04,700
the kopitiam breakfast was over the
limit before the toast was finished.

121
00:08:04,700 --> 00:08:09,517
The science began to move in
1999, when a JAMA study following

122
00:08:09,597 --> 00:08:14,967
more than 117,000 men and women
found no difference in cardiovascular

123
00:08:15,047 --> 00:08:19,944
risk between eating one egg a
week and one egg a day in healthy

124
00:08:20,024 --> 00:08:25,000
people (people with diabetes were
the exception in that study).

125
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:31,047
In 2002 the Heart Association quietly
dropped its specific egg restriction,

126
00:08:31,127 --> 00:08:34,150
though it kept the 300-milligram cap.

127
00:08:34,150 --> 00:08:39,197
In 2013 its joint guideline with
the American College of Cardiology

128
00:08:39,277 --> 00:08:44,171
concluded there was "insufficient
evidence" that lowering dietary

129
00:08:44,251 --> 00:08:49,225
cholesterol reduces LDL cholesterol
at all — and dropped the cap.

130
00:08:49,225 --> 00:08:52,935
In February 2015 the federal
Dietary Guidelines Advisory

131
00:08:53,015 --> 00:08:56,725
Committee went further in
words that were widely quoted:

132
00:08:56,805 --> 00:09:01,056
cholesterol was "not a nutrient
of concern for overconsumption",

133
00:09:01,136 --> 00:09:05,320
and the evidence showed no
appreciable relationship between the

134
00:09:05,400 --> 00:09:09,325
cholesterol people eat and the
cholesterol in their blood.

135
00:09:09,325 --> 00:09:12,837
The popular version of
that story — "in 2015,

136
00:09:12,917 --> 00:09:18,825
America declared eggs fine again" —
is too simple, and the record says so.

137
00:09:18,825 --> 00:09:22,720
The final 2015 to 2020
Dietary Guidelines removed

138
00:09:22,800 --> 00:09:25,316
the number but kept the caution,

139
00:09:25,396 --> 00:09:31,075
advising Americans to eat "as little
dietary cholesterol as possible".

140
00:09:31,075 --> 00:09:33,800
And the argument did not end.

141
00:09:33,800 --> 00:09:37,794
In March 2019 a JAMA analysis
pooling six American cohorts —

142
00:09:37,874 --> 00:09:42,344
29,615 people followed for a median
of seventeen and a half years —

143
00:09:42,424 --> 00:09:46,894
reported that each additional 300
milligrams of dietary cholesterol

144
00:09:46,974 --> 00:09:50,765
a day was associated with a
higher risk of cardiovascular

145
00:09:50,845 --> 00:09:54,839
disease and death, and each
additional half egg a day with a

146
00:09:54,919 --> 00:09:59,253
smaller but real increase (though
the egg associations faded once

147
00:09:59,333 --> 00:10:03,395
dietary cholesterol itself was
accounted for, and the study's

148
00:10:03,475 --> 00:10:08,025
critics noted that diet had been
measured only once, at the start).

149
00:10:08,025 --> 00:10:12,623
In December 2019 the Heart
Association's Science Advisory settled

150
00:10:12,703 --> 00:10:16,798
where the evidence had landed:
no specific numeric target,

151
00:10:16,878 --> 00:10:21,908
an emphasis on the whole dietary
pattern — healthy people could include

152
00:10:21,988 --> 00:10:26,450
up to one whole egg a day, and
healthy older people up to two.

153
00:10:26,450 --> 00:10:31,475
In 2020 a BMJ analysis of three
cohorts and a meta-analysis

154
00:10:31,555 --> 00:10:37,013
covering 1.72 million people found
no association between eating

155
00:10:37,093 --> 00:10:42,025
at least one egg a day and
cardiovascular disease at all.

156
00:10:42,025 --> 00:10:45,498
These are observational
studies — associations,

157
00:10:45,578 --> 00:10:48,450
not proofs — and their authors say so.

158
00:10:48,450 --> 00:10:52,968
The fair summary is narrower than
either camp's headline: a hard cap

159
00:10:53,048 --> 00:10:56,891
for forty-five years, then no
cap with cautionary wording,

160
00:10:56,971 --> 00:11:01,975
then pattern-based advice, with large
studies still landing on both sides.

161
00:11:01,975 --> 00:11:05,125
The guidance swung for half a century.

162
00:11:05,125 --> 00:11:07,425
The saucer did not.

163
00:11:07,425 --> 00:11:10,261
Singapore's published advice, notably,

164
00:11:10,341 --> 00:11:12,950
did not follow the American swing.

165
00:11:12,950 --> 00:11:16,056
On HealthHub, the Health
Promotion Board's portal,

166
00:11:16,136 --> 00:11:20,515
a dietitian's answer updated on the
22nd of December 2021 concedes the

167
00:11:20,595 --> 00:11:24,593
modern point — dietary cholesterol,
it says, has a "minor effect

168
00:11:24,673 --> 00:11:28,543
on serum cholesterol" — and then
states the old limits anyway:

169
00:11:28,623 --> 00:11:32,302
the Health Promotion Board
advises less than 300 milligrams

170
00:11:32,382 --> 00:11:36,061
of cholesterol a day, and fewer
than four egg yolks a week,

171
00:11:36,141 --> 00:11:39,900
with the real concern directed
at saturated and trans fats.

172
00:11:39,900 --> 00:11:43,314
The Board's My Healthy
Plate materials of the same

173
00:11:43,394 --> 00:11:47,018
period repeat the four-yolk
figure, and the Singapore

174
00:11:47,098 --> 00:11:50,582
Heart Foundation's table,
citing Ministry of Health

175
00:11:50,662 --> 00:11:54,575
guidelines from 2016, keeps
the 300-milligram limit too.

176
00:11:54,575 --> 00:11:58,464
Whether the Board has formally
revised the advice since

177
00:11:58,544 --> 00:12:02,360
those 2021 pages could not
be confirmed for this post;

178
00:12:02,440 --> 00:12:06,329
what is documented is that
Singapore's printed guidance

179
00:12:06,409 --> 00:12:10,450
long outlasted the American
original it was modelled on.

180
00:12:10,450 --> 00:12:14,554
Through all of it, Singaporeans
went on ordering the two eggs —

181
00:12:14,634 --> 00:12:17,609
on the Board's own
numbers, a single breakfast

182
00:12:17,689 --> 00:12:21,475
saucer holds more than a whole
day's advised cholesterol.

183
00:12:21,475 --> 00:12:25,272
It is fair to ask whether the
coffee itself is harmful —

184
00:12:25,352 --> 00:12:28,675
and it turns out to have
had a scare of its own.

185
00:12:28,675 --> 00:12:33,350
Kopitiam coffee is not
ordinary coffee to begin with.

186
00:12:33,350 --> 00:12:37,690
The Nanyang roast, as the Heritage
Board records it, is eight parts

187
00:12:37,770 --> 00:12:40,262
beans to two parts sugar and margarine,

188
00:12:40,342 --> 00:12:46,001
roasted twenty-five minutes at 180 degrees,
with salt added midway and sugar at the end

189
00:12:46,081 --> 00:12:50,091
for a caramelised finish (the
National Museum's catalogue says

190
00:12:50,171 --> 00:12:54,525
butter where the Heritage Board
says margarine — the split again).

191
00:12:54,525 --> 00:12:57,449
The brew is strained
through a cloth sock and

192
00:12:57,529 --> 00:13:01,000
poured back and forth between
two long-spouted jugs.

193
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:05,677
Even a kopi-o kosong is therefore
not quite a plain black coffee:

194
00:13:05,757 --> 00:13:09,050
the beans arrived
pre-sweetened by the roast.

195
00:13:09,050 --> 00:13:13,298
In 1991 the International
Agency for Research on Cancer,

196
00:13:13,378 --> 00:13:16,854
the World Health
Organization's cancer agency,

197
00:13:16,934 --> 00:13:22,033
classified coffee in Group 2B —
"possibly carcinogenic to humans" —

198
00:13:22,113 --> 00:13:25,900
on limited evidence linking
it to bladder cancer.

199
00:13:25,900 --> 00:13:28,800
There it sat for twenty-five years.

200
00:13:28,800 --> 00:13:33,296
In June 2016, after reviewing
more than a thousand studies,

201
00:13:33,376 --> 00:13:39,656
the agency moved coffee to Group 3, "not
classifiable as to its carcinogenicity" —

202
00:13:39,736 --> 00:13:42,216
no conclusive effect at any site,

203
00:13:42,296 --> 00:13:47,725
with the 1991 bladder evidence
weakened by smoking's confounding role.

204
00:13:47,725 --> 00:13:50,763
Group 3 is an absence-of-evidence verdict,

205
00:13:50,843 --> 00:13:55,000
not a certificate of safety,
and precision matters here.

206
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:59,112
In the same evaluation, the
agency classified drinking

207
00:13:59,192 --> 00:14:03,148
very hot beverages — above
65 degrees — as Group 2A,

208
00:14:03,228 --> 00:14:07,575
"probably carcinogenic": the
temperature, not the drink.

209
00:14:07,575 --> 00:14:12,714
A 2017 umbrella review in the BMJ,
covering over two hundred meta-analyses,

210
00:14:12,794 --> 00:14:15,707
concluded that coffee
consumption was "more

211
00:14:15,787 --> 00:14:19,743
often associated with benefit
than harm", with the largest

212
00:14:19,823 --> 00:14:22,875
risk reductions at three
to four cups a day —

213
00:14:22,955 --> 00:14:28,303
while cautioning that the evidence is
observational and mostly of low quality,

214
00:14:28,383 --> 00:14:32,350
and that the associations ran
the other way in pregnancy.

215
00:14:32,350 --> 00:14:37,525
The live issue in a modern kopi-o
is not the coffee but the sugar.

216
00:14:37,525 --> 00:14:43,482
The Health Promotion Board's food database
figures, as reported in secondary analyses,

217
00:14:43,562 --> 00:14:47,483
put a 250-millilitre kopi-o
at about 11.7 grams of sugar,

218
00:14:47,563 --> 00:14:51,344
against none at all in a
kosong; other published tables

219
00:14:51,424 --> 00:14:56,257
give different figures for the same
drinks, so treat any single number

220
00:14:56,337 --> 00:15:00,900
as an approximation with a source
attached, not a fact of nature.

221
00:15:00,900 --> 00:15:04,536
What is official is the
grading: under Nutri-Grade,

222
00:15:04,616 --> 00:15:09,929
the Board's own worked examples grade
a kopi or teh O kosong A, siu dai B,

223
00:15:10,009 --> 00:15:14,593
the standard cup C, and ga dai
D. The labels became mandatory on

224
00:15:14,673 --> 00:15:19,329
pre-packaged drinks in December
2022 and were extended to freshly

225
00:15:19,409 --> 00:15:24,000
prepared drinks — the kopitiam
cup included — in December 2023.

226
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:27,281
On government premises,
coffee and tea are now

227
00:15:27,361 --> 00:15:30,575
served kosong by default,
sugar on the side.

228
00:15:30,575 --> 00:15:34,746
The kopi-o has not been banned,
taxed or portioned by the week;

229
00:15:34,826 --> 00:15:39,550
it has been graded, and the grading
points at the spoon, not the bean.

230
00:15:39,550 --> 00:15:43,975
Singapore eats eggs on a
scale the scare never dented.

231
00:15:43,975 --> 00:15:47,737
By the Singapore Food
Agency's 2024 statistics,

232
00:15:47,817 --> 00:15:53,622
the country consumed roughly 2,259
million eggs in a year — on the order

233
00:15:53,702 --> 00:15:58,445
of 370 eggs per person — with
local farms supplying about a

234
00:15:58,525 --> 00:16:03,675
third and the rest imported,
mainly from Malaysia and Thailand.

235
00:16:03,675 --> 00:16:09,401
That dependence has shown itself exactly
once in the breakfast's modern history:

236
00:16:09,481 --> 00:16:12,158
in 2004, during the bird flu outbreak,

237
00:16:12,238 --> 00:16:16,375
Singapore banned Malaysian
poultry imports for six weeks.

238
00:16:16,375 --> 00:16:21,321
Killiney Kopitiam, which typically
used up to 4,000 fresh eggs a day,

239
00:16:21,401 --> 00:16:25,472
received less than half its
supply, reserved what eggs it

240
00:16:25,552 --> 00:16:29,551
had for kaya, watched prices
spike from 12 to 45 cents —

241
00:16:29,631 --> 00:16:34,875
and took the soft-boiled eggs off the
menu across its fourteen branches.

242
00:16:34,875 --> 00:16:38,086
The only thing that has
ever actually removed

243
00:16:38,166 --> 00:16:41,750
the two eggs from the
saucer was not a guideline.

244
00:16:41,750 --> 00:16:43,750
It was a shortage.

245
00:16:43,750 --> 00:16:47,509
So the set endures, graded
but intact: the eggs steeping

246
00:16:47,589 --> 00:16:51,348
in their pot behind the
counter, the toast on the grill,

247
00:16:51,428 --> 00:16:54,650
the sock going back and
forth between the jugs.

248
00:16:54,650 --> 00:16:57,959
It has been heritage-listed,
cholesterol-capped,

249
00:16:58,039 --> 00:17:02,761
carcinogen-listed, de-listed, and
Nutri-Graded, and it is still what

250
00:17:02,841 --> 00:17:07,281
it was when Kheng Hoe Heng opened
in 1919 — because a breakfast,

251
00:17:07,361 --> 00:17:10,750
unlike a guideline, is
not revised by committee.

252
00:17:10,750 --> 00:17:14,224
It is revised by nobody,
one morning at a time,

253
00:17:14,304 --> 00:17:16,950
by the people who keep ordering it.

254
00:17:16,950 --> 00:17:19,187
Where it fits in the bigger story:

255
00:17:19,267 --> 00:17:23,208
Singapore's most ordinary
breakfast turns out to be a small

256
00:17:23,288 --> 00:17:26,900
monument to the difference
between custom and advice.

257
00:17:26,900 --> 00:17:31,472
The advice was expert, official
and — by its own later admission —

258
00:17:31,552 --> 00:17:33,939
built on evidence too weak to hold;

259
00:17:34,019 --> 00:17:37,825
it governed the egg for fifty
years and then withdrew.

260
00:17:37,825 --> 00:17:42,450
The custom asked for no evidence
at all, and outlasted it.

261
00:17:42,450 --> 00:17:47,250
The lesser-known thing is not that
the two eggs survived the scare.

262
00:17:47,250 --> 00:17:51,025
It is that nobody ever
decided they should.
